Here is a simple example of a C++ program that sorts a large array using threads:
```c
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

const int NUM_THREADS = 4;  // Number of threads to use

// Function to sort a portion of the array using std::sort
void sortArray(int* arr, int start, int end) {
  std::sort(arr + start, arr + end);
}

int main() {
  int arr[10000];  // The array to sort

  // Initialize the array with random values
  for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
    arr[i] = rand();
  }

  // Create an array of threads
  std::thread threads[NUM_THREADS];

  // Divide the array into NUM_THREADS equal-sized portions and sort each
  // portion in a separate thread
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; ++i) {
    int start = (10000 / NUM_THREADS) * i;
    int end = start + (10000 / NUM_THREADS);
    threads[i] = std::thread(sortArray, arr, start, end);
  }

  // Wait for all threads to finish
  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; ++i) {
    threads[i].join();
  }

  // Print the sorted array
  for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
    std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
  }

  return 0;
}
```
In this program, we use the `std::thread` class to create and manage threads. The `sortArray` function sorts a portion of the array using the `std::sort` algorithm. In `main`, we create an array of threads and assign each thread a portion of the array to sort. Once all of the threads have finished, we join them and print the sorted array.

